<resources>
    <dimen name="overscan_horizontal">27dp</dimen>
    <dimen name="overscan_vertical">48dp</dimen>

    <fraction name="card_scale_default">100%</fraction>
    <!-- How much bigger the focused card is drawn. Each card reserves a band of exactly this much
         around itself (cardFocusGrowthRoom) and grows into it, so the growth is never clipped and
         never lands on a neighbour, whatever this is set to.

         106% was too small to read across a room, which is the whole job. The ceiling is not the
         gutter any more but the resting layout: the band is spent whether a card is focused or
         not, so raising this pushes every card further apart and fits fewer on a row.

         BrowseGridFragment reads this too, to reserve the same band in library grids. That
         coupling is deliberate, and still right: the band is part of the card's footprint. -->
    <fraction name="card_scale_focus">110%</fraction>

    <!-- Most of the gutter is now the band each card reserves for its own growth (see
         cardFocusGrowthRoom), which is proportional to the card and so already scales with it. On a
         185dp card that band is 9dp a side, giving 22dp between two resting cards. This is only the
         floor under it, for cards small enough that their band comes to almost nothing. -->
    <dimen name="lb_browse_item_horizontal_spacing">4dp</dimen>

    <!-- Leanback is told not to scale anything, and the growth in card_scale_focus is applied
         inside the card instead.

         Leanback scales the whole item view, and on a row that shows its titles underneath the
         cards that view is the card AND its two lines of text. So the focused card's title grew
         and slid down with it while its neighbours' titles stayed where they were, and no two
         titles in a row shared a baseline — the row rippled as you moved along it. Scaling the
         artwork alone leaves the words where they were put. See ItemCard.

         Every card that leanback lays out therefore has to draw its own focus state. ItemCard and
         GridButtonPresenter do; anything else added to a row must too, or it will show nothing at
         all when focused. -->
    <fraction name="lb_focus_zoom_factor_small">@fraction/card_scale_default</fraction>
    <fraction name="lb_focus_zoom_factor_xsmall">@fraction/card_scale_default</fraction>
    <fraction name="lb_focus_zoom_factor_medium">@fraction/card_scale_default</fraction>
    <fraction name="lb_focus_zoom_factor_large">@fraction/card_scale_default</fraction>
</resources>
